window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.2","title":"The Binomial Distribution","goal":"Use the binomial distribution for the lesson's drug that works for 70% of patients (10 patients): exactly, at most and at least, plus mean, SD and a simulation check.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Exactly 7 respond","text":"binom.pmf(k, n, p) gives P(X = k), one bar of the distribution. We also compute the lesson's formula C(n, k) x p^k x (1 - p)^(n - k) by hand to see that they agree.","code":"import math\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import binom\n\nn, p = 10, 0.7\n\nby_hand = math.comb(n, 7) * p**7 * (1 - p)**3\nprint(f'formula   : {by_hand:.4f}')\nprint(f'binom.pmf : {binom.pmf(7, n, p):.4f}')","out":"formula   : 0.2668\nbinom.pmf : 0.2668","figs":[]},{"title":"At most, more than, at least","text":"cdf(k) is P(X <= k), 'at most k'. sf(k) is the survival function P(X > k), 'more than k'. So 'at least 9' is sf(8), which is the same as 1 minus cdf(8).","code":"print(f'P(X <= 6)  at most 6      = {binom.cdf(6, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X > 8)   more than 8      = {binom.sf(8, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 9)  at least 9       = {1 - binom.cdf(8, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(9) + P(10) added by hand  = {binom.pmf(9, n, p) + binom.pmf(10, n, p):.4f}')","out":"P(X <= 6)  at most 6      = 0.3504\nP(X > 8)   more than 8      = 0.1493\nP(X >= 9)  at least 9       = 0.1493\nP(9) + P(10) added by hand  = 0.1493","figs":[]},{"title":"Mean and standard deviation","text":"The shortcuts are mean = n x p and SD = square root of n x p x (1 - p). SciPy's stats method returns the same values from the distribution itself.","code":"print(f'mean = n*p = {n * p:.1f}')\nprint(f'variance   = {n * p * (1 - p):.2f}')\nprint(f'SD         = {math.sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)):.3f}')\n\nm, v = binom.stats(n, p)\nprint(f'scipy      : mean {float(m):.1f}, variance {float(v):.2f}')","out":"mean = n*p = 7.0\nvariance   = 2.10\nSD         = 1.449\nscipy      : mean 7.0, variance 2.10","figs":[]},{"title":"Check by simulation","text":"Run the 10-patient trial 100,000 times with a seeded generator and count how many responders each trial has. The simulated shares should be close to the exact probabilities.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nresponders = rng.binomial(n, p, size=100_000)\n\nprint(f'simulated P(X = 7)  = {(responders == 7).mean():.4f}  (exact {binom.pmf(7, n, p):.4f})')\nprint(f'simulated P(X >= 9) = {(responders >= 9).mean():.4f}  (exact {binom.sf(8, n, p):.4f})')\nprint(f'simulated mean {responders.mean():.3f}, SD {responders.std():.3f}')","out":"simulated P(X = 7)  = 0.2679  (exact 0.2668)\nsimulated P(X >= 9) = 0.1485  (exact 0.1493)\nsimulated mean 6.997, SD 1.450","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"n <- 10; p <- 0.7\ndbinom(7, n, p)                       # exactly 7\npbinom(6, n, p)                       # at most 6\npbinom(8, n, p, lower.tail = FALSE)   # more than 8 = at least 9\nn * p\nsqrt(n * p * (1 - p))\n\nset.seed(42)\nx <- rbinom(100000, n, p)\nmean(x == 7)","expect":"P(X = 7) = 0.2668; P(X <= 6) = 0.3504; P(X >= 9) = 0.1493; mean 7; sd 1.449; simulated P(X = 7) close to 0.2668","note":"dbinom, pbinom and rbinom are R's pmf, cdf and random draws. lower.tail = FALSE gives the survival probability P(X > k). The simulation is close to the exact value, not identical to Python's."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (Function group: PDF & Noncentral PDF, CDF & Noncentral CDF, Random Numbers)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE exact7 = PDF.BINOM(7, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE atmost6 = CDF.BINOM(6, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE atleast9 = 1 - CDF.BINOM(8, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE mean_x = 10 * 0.7.\nCOMPUTE sd_x = SQRT(10 * 0.7 * 0.3).\nFORMATS exact7 atmost6 atleast9 sd_x (F8.4).\nLIST VARIABLES=exact7 atmost6 atleast9 mean_x sd_x.","note":"PDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the pmf and CDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is P(X <= k). For a simulation use COMPUTE x = RV.BINOM(10, 0.7) on many cases; the result is close to, not identical to, Python's."},"stata":{"code":"display binomialp(10, 7, 0.7)\ndisplay binomial(10, 6, 0.7)\ndisplay binomialtail(10, 9, 0.7)\ndisplay 10 * 0.7\ndisplay sqrt(10 * 0.7 * 0.3)\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 100000\ngen x = rbinomial(10, 0.7)\ngen is7 = (x == 7)\nsummarize is7","note":"In Stata binomialp(n, k, p) is P(X = k), binomial(n, k, p) is P(X <= k) and binomialtail(n, k, p) is P(X >= k), so 'at least 9' uses k = 9. The simulated mean of is7 is close to 0.2668, not identical to Python's."},"try":["Change p to 0.5 and predict where the most likely count moves, then print binom.pmf for k = 0 to 10.","A coin is flipped 100 times and gives 62 heads. Compute binom.sf(61, 100, 0.5) and compare with the lesson's 0.0105."]});
